en mostly looted there are  some subtler clues about the nature of  the world that you might notice here  like the fact that the items with  noisier packaging like bags of chips  remain on the shelves we also see two  children trying very hard to be quiet  but that the youngest child isn't quite  as careful as the oldest child but as  far as we know they could be playing a  game we learned through visual cues and  sound design that Regan the  oldest girl is deaf there's an  interesting bit of sound design used for  this since the sound design for this  scene and a lot of the movie in general  is incredibly quiet portraying the  audible perspective of a deaf person  might be difficult you could make things  completely silent but instead they opted  for a more obvious audible cue you're  going to have a hard time hearing this  if you're not wearing headphones or in a  theater with a good sound system but  when we're within reagan's perspective  the noise level is actually louder than  the rest of the scene but instead of  clearly hearing the noises in the room  all we hear is a deep muffled hum this  is also why we need the youngest child  to be noisily running around if the  entire family was completely silent for  this scene there would be nothing for us  to not hear from Reagan's perspective  another thing I love about how this  scene unfolds is the layered  introduction of a lot of the family  we're tracking the movement and action  of one of the family members and then  catch a glimpse of a new character in  the background it first happens here  this shot simply and effectively  communicates that one child is sick and  that the family is here for medicine the  camera racking focus implies an  association between the two subjects and  simultaneously we get another background  reveal of a new character the mother and  see her trying to be extremely quiet  which Clues us into the fact that the  children aren't just playing a game part  of what's great about film as 