 the transition to the second  part of the sequence five minutes in  we've been introduced to the characters  developed some of the relationships and  learned some of the basic rules of the  world so the last half of the sequence  is about two things creating the first  real moment of suspense in the film and  setting the stakes high a quiet place is  mostly a suspense film and there are  several ways of effectively generating  suspense you can generate suspense using  a feeling of uncertainty to create  anxiety in the audience but oddly you  can also generate a feeling of suspense  by creating certainty that an undesired  outcome will occur a quiet place often  uses dramatic irony to create this  suspense of certainty the shot of the  youngest child picking up the batteries  creates dramatic irony the audience  knows the batteries will go into the toy  and the toy will make a sound because we  know the movie wouldn't have put  emphasis on this detail if it wasn't  going to happen but the rest of the  family is completely unaware of the  impending disaster so we're left waiting  for something to happen that we don't  want to see happen and that suspense is  built further by drawing things out with  a simple scene of the family walking  through the woods in this scene we  learned the use of the sand we saw  earlier and that the family lives a good  distance from the town the music is more  mellow and sad than it is tense which  builds the dramatic irony the music  matches how the characters feel not the  audience's knowledge reinforcing the  idea that the characters are  unsuspecting of what the audience knows  will happen the music finally dies down  and we're bracing ourselves for the  sound the content looks on the mother  and daughter's face make it all the  worse for the audience note that by this  point we don't need to see a shot of the  toy making noise because we know what's  going to happen the suspense is extended  a moment longer by first showing the toy 